# config/subsys/Kconfig.ota — OTA firmware updates
#
# OTA slot size limit.  Signature enforcement is
# OTA_SIG_MANDATORY (below; the scheme is Ed25519, the
# only one implemented).  The anti-rollback floors (BOOTMETA
# min_fw_version, min_sec_epoch) are always on; where the epoch floor is
# also held is OTA_FLOOR_* (below).

config OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
    bool "OTA images must be signed (refuse unsigned updates)" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      Default: y on every build except BUILD_TYPE_DEV (release-safe),
      whatever the SECURITY posture. When y, the OTA subsystem rejects any firmware image that does
      not carry a valid Ed25519 signature file (KERN_*.SIG on FAT).
      Recommended: set to y whenever SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED = y so
      the signing chain is unbroken end-to-end.  A BUILD_TYPE_DEV build
      starts on only for the SECURITY postures but general; a
      BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it on and cannot turn it off.

choice
    prompt "OTA anti-rollback floor (min_sec_epoch) backend"
    default OTA_FLOOR_BOOTMETA
    help
      Where the epoch floor of the whole-system update is held. A manifest
      whose sec_epoch is below the floor is refused whatever its
      signature, and the floor only ever goes up, when a boot is
      confirmed.

      BOOTMETA: the floor is a field of the BOOTMETA.A/.B records on the
      FAT volume (CRC and sequence number, no secret): whoever can write
      the volume (it is exported over USB mass storage) can write an older
      record back and lower the floor, and the signature check is then the
      only gate left. TPM NV: the floor is also a 4-byte NV index of the
      TPM, defined once and raised only upward (a read comes first and a
      value at or below the stored one is never written); the floor in
      force is the higher of the two, so replacing BOOTMETA no longer
      lowers it. The index is an ordinary one with a password the kernel
      derives (see kernel/src/boot/tpm.rs), so it is as safe as that
      password is secret; a policy bound to PCRs is the next step. With
      no TPM found, or one that fails, BOOTMETA's floor holds alone and
      the boot says so. (A replay-protected eMMC partition, RPMB, is the
      other hardware floor this choice has a place for; none is
      implemented.)

    config OTA_FLOOR_BOOTMETA
        bool "BOOTMETA: the FAT volume's boot record only"
    config OTA_FLOOR_TPM_NV
        bool "TPM NV: BOOTMETA and a TPM NV index, the higher wins"
        depends on TPM
endchoice

config OTA_MAX_IMAGE_SIZE_MB
    int "Maximum OTA image size (MiB)"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      Hard limit on firmware image size accepted by the OTA receiver.
      Images larger than this are rejected before writing to FAT.
      This is the ONLY size ceiling in the OTA path (crates/core/ota's
      `OTA_MAX_IMAGE_SIZE` reads this symbol via azos_limits, and
      `tools/ota_send.py` reads the same `.config` this expands to, so
      neither can drift from it). Secure boot verification used to carry
      a second, smaller, disconnected ceiling (a 2 MiB `.bss` buffer to
      hold a whole image); that buffer is gone as of 2026-09-26 (U09-14)
      now that verification streams the hash instead of buffering the
      image, so raising this value costs no extra `.bss`.

      Cost: none (+0 B of RAM measured for 8 -> 9 and -> 16 MiB, both ISAs).
